            <ArticleTitle>Factors Associated with Increased Risk for Clinical Insomnia in Patients with Postherpetic Neuralgia: A Retrospective Cross-Sectional Study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1917-1922</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the risk factors associated with clinical insomnia in postherpetic neuralgia (PHN) patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective cross-sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient department for interventional pain management at a university hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 111 patients with PHN satisfied the study inclusion criteria and were included in the analyses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Insomnia Severity Index (ISI) was used to determine the presence of clinical insomnia (ISI score ≥ 15). Patient demographics, pain-related factors, and rash severity and location were evaluated with logistic regression analysis to identify risk factors of clinical insomnia among patients with PHN.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In total, 50.5% of patients reported mild to severe insomnia symptoms (ISI score ≥ 8) after pain development. Moderate to severe clinical insomnia (ISI score ≥ 15) was observed in 30.6% of PHN patients. Multivariate logistic regression analyses revealed that high pain intensity was the strongest predictor of clinical insomnia (odds ratio (OR) = 12.417, 95% confidence interval (CI): 2.990-51.561, P = 0.001). However, presence of mechanical allodynia (OR = 4.263, 95% CI: 1.040-17.481, P = 0.034) and high anxiety and depression level (OR = 4.452, 95% CI: 1.201-16.508, P = 0.026; OR = 6.975, 95% CI: 1.425-34.138, P = 0.017) were also significantly associated with clinical insomnia after adjusting for pain score. Clinical insomnia was not significantly related to age, gender, rash severity, or location of skin lesion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Insomnia should be addressed as an important part of pain management in PHN patients with these risk factors, especially in patients with severe pain.</AbstractText>
